agdash on Ubuntu
================

The service runs as your own user and reads only your sessions. Installing the
package starts nothing; start it for your user:

    agdash install          start the service now and at every login
    agdash status           whether it runs, and what it holds
    agdash uninstall        stop starting it at login; your data stays

On a server you log out of, a user service stops at logout unless lingering is
enabled for your account. agdash does not change that; to keep it running:

    loginctl enable-linger "$USER"

With no display, open the UI in a browser on your own computer. On the server:

    agdash open

prints a link that carries the service's local token. Anyone who opens it can
read your sessions, so keep it to yourself. Then, on your computer, forward the
port and open the link:

    ssh -N -L 8765:127.0.0.1:8765 <server>

The service listens on 127.0.0.1 only, and refuses requests from other web pages.
